  "body": "A user who puts `camcraft-cli` on their PATH with a symlink (the usual way on macOS, where the binary is inside `CamCraft.app/Contents/MacOS/`) cannot post. MCP clients (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex, Cursor) are set up with such a path.\n\nReproduced on v0.24.0 (macOS, `camcraft-0.24.0-macos-universal.zip`), with one project:\n\n| Started as | `process <project> -o out.nc` |\n|---|---|\n| `/Applications/CamCraft.app/Contents/MacOS/camcraft-cli` | rc 0, posted |\n| a symlink to that file, in another folder | rc 1 |\n| a copy of that file, in another folder | rc 1 |\n\nThe error of the symlink:\n\n```\nERROR camcraft_app::posting::files: shipped posts file tormach.cps: no posts folder found - the program needs its assets/posts directory beside the executable (looked in: <folder of the symlink>/assets/posts)\ncamcraft-cli: post: built-in post Tormach PathPilot: no posts folder found ...\n```\n\nCause: the posts folder is found beside the path of the started executable, without resolving the symlink to the real file.\n\nDone means:\n- A symlinked `camcraft-cli` finds the shipped posts (resolve the executable path with `std::fs::canonicalize` before the lookup), with a test.\n- A plain copy still refuses with a clear message, as now. That message names the setting or environment variable to point at an assets folder, if there is one; otherwise it says to run the binary from the install folder or to use a symlink.\n- The Windows and Linux layouts are checked the same way, and the result is recorded on this issue.\n",
